ion`): `sdpa` gives `auto`'s losses and weights where the sites' references are plain SDPA (F5-TTS, Qwen3-TTS, VoxCPM); FlashAttention and xFormers stay within 1e-3 of the losses, Qwen3-TTS within 0.25 % at the first step before its bf16 trajectory wanders (as any change of rounding makes it); peaks and step times move by under 7 %. **Fallbacks** on the real kernels (a bool mask into flash, head dim 80 or a gradient into SageAttention, fp32 into either, xFormers that does not import) fall back with their reason and give the reference's result; strict mode refuses them.","locale":"en"},"13812":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#attention-backends-on-a-gpu","content":"**Found and fixed:** SDPA's math kernel called directly adds a bool mask as numbers (the `math` backend and Fish Speech's decoding steps; see Fish Speech); SageAttention faulted on any GPU but the current one; Qwen's attention under a policy left grouped heads with a mask to SDPA's math kernel (OmniVoice 0.71× and other bits under every policy), now repeated as its reference does.","locale":"en"},"13813":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#attention-backends-on-a-gpu","content":"**Decided:** `fast` on NVIDIA is FlashAttention for fp16/bf16 calls over padded keys and SDPA for everything else (`PADDED_CHAIN`); SageAttention and xFormers leave its chain and stay available by name; autoregressive sites refuse quantised backends (`approx_ok=False`); `auto` stays the default (D-A6: `sdpa` gives the same audio where the reference is plain SDPA, no faster). The doctor's flash-attn command says that on torch 2.8 it and xFormers exclude each other.","locale":"en"},"13814":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"Measured on an RTX 3090 (2026-10-18), each model capped (`compute.vram_limit_mb`) so its decoder streams, warm requests against the code before, three runs a case, the audio compared.","locale":"en"},"13815":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"**Reloading** a component unloaded to RAM copied 7–9.8 GB/s, 52–73 % of the 13.45 GB/s the card takes from page-locked memory: the kept host copies were pageable, so each copy waited for the one before. They are now page-locked in place as the component loads (`runtime/pinning.py`, see Memory); offload, the first load and peak memory are unchanged:","locale":"en"},"13816":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"Reload (whole GPU)","locale":"en"},"13817":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"MB","locale":"en"},"13818":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"Pageable","locale":"en"},"13819":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"Pinned in place","locale":"en"},"13820":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#partly-loaded-models","content":"CosyVoice 2","locale":"en"},"13821":{"id":"/en/docs/development-per