hey are downloaded with the first model that needs them (the job's steps list them); a model whose shared files are missing is **Partly downloaded**."],[12193,"They bring version conflicts, platform limits or global state; tokenizers come from `tokenizers`, `tiktoken` and `sentencepiece`, and ONNX models' weights are converted into torch modules by an in-tree protobuf reader"],[12199,"They support only some platforms; the default path must work everywhere with unchanged results"],[11702,"Third-party modules it may import"],[15994,"This machine's devices, each GPU's memory, and the torch in use with its build, such as `torch 2.8.0 (cu128)`. When torch is missing or its build does not suit this computer, it names the right build and shows the command that installs it (`mizuoto doctor` says the same)"],[11160,"This page covers the shared conventions and common workflows; every command's options are in the Command reference, settings and environment variables in Configuration and environment variables."],[11698,"This page gives the whole picture: the packages and what each may import, the layer rules `tests/test_architecture.py` enforces, how `build_services()` assembles the services, the path of one synthesis request from the browser to an engine and back, the core's one door to model code, how engines, models, voices and remote hosts are organised, how the data folder carries over between versions, and the shared infrastructure: settings, events, jobs, errors. What happens inside the runtime is in Runtime; routes and commands are in API and CLI."],[10121,"This page groups the commands by purpose; `mizuoto --help` and each group's help list them alphabetically. Conventions and exit codes are in the Overview, settings in Configuration and environment variables."],[12015,"This page lists the conventions a change to Mizuoto must follow. Most of them are held by tests or checks: `python scripts/dev.py check` passing is the least a change needs before it is merged. The design behind each convention is in Architecture and Design references and decisions."],[17053,"This page takes the shortest path. Installation and First run have the details of each step."],[16797,"This section explains the Mizuoto web UI screen by screen. If you have not installed or started Mizuoto yet, read Quick start first. Every action in the UI has a `mizuoto` command too; see Command line."],[15041,"This section is for people who want to change Mizuoto: fix a bug, add an engine, a training method, a screen or an API route. It describes what the code **is** now, the rules it keeps and why, not how to use it (for that, see the guides and the command line)."],[15852,"This section records, engine by engine, what that took, where an engine deliberately differs, how it uses memory and runs fast, how it trains, and what the comparison found. Which engine to choose and what each offers is in Engines in depth; how an engine is written and registered is in Adding an engine."],[14184,"This site lives in `site/`. It is built on fumadocs (TanStack Start + Vite) with bun into static files (`.output/public`). `.github/workflows/site.yml` publishes it to GitHub Pages: on a push to `main` that changes `site/` (or by hand) it runs the site's checks, builds it with `VITE_BASE_PATH=/Mizuoto/` (Pages serves it under `https://licyk.github.io/Mizuoto/`; the router and the search index follow Vite's base), adds `.nojekyll`, and force-pushes the output to the `gh-pages` branch over SSH with the `GITEE_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY` secret (a key with write access to the repository). The repository's Pages settings serve that branch. `site/vercel.json` is ready for a Vercel deployment at the root path."],[12249,"Those change the recipes beyond what the presets promise"],[16032,"Three filters at the top, which combine:"],[14556,"Three refinements came from the first full run, and the tier's rule above includes them:"],[9030,"Three scripts, run from a source checkout, make and publish a mirror: `scripts/mirror_models.py` lays out the models installed on this machine, publishes the result and checks it; `scripts/mirror_fetch.py` downloads the whole catalogue into the mirror's layout; `scripts/mirror_readme.py` writes the repository's card and licence files. Publishing is the mirror owner's step: the weights keep their own licences, and `MIRROR.json` names each file's origin."],[11735,"Three subprocess tests: `import mizuoto.core.context` loads neither torch, fastapi nor typer; `import mizuoto.cli.app` loads neither torch nor fastapi; importing the engine contract, the request context, the dummy engine, `core.inference.local` and `core.inference.runtime` loads no torch. That keeps `mizuoto version` and `mizuoto --help` fast and lets the core's tests run on the dummy engine."],[15232,"Through the stack each side computes its own voice prompt, and CAM++'s embedding is the one part not bit-exact: it differs from onnxruntime's by at most 7e-6 (cosine 0.999999999997). The flow turns that into a mel change of at most 2.7e-3, and HiFT's sine source integrates the F0 into phase (a float32 cumulative sum over the whole utterance, nine harmonics), so the error grows along the utterance: on a CosyVoice 3 English clone the SNR per half second falls from 66 to 20 dB. Upstream is just as sensitive. Its own vocoder chain fed Mizuoto's embedding gives exactly Mizuoto's audio; its own onnxruntime CAM++ with 8 threads instead of 1 gives 31 dB; random perturbations of that size give 20–33 dB. CosyVoice 2/3's language model never uses the speaker embedding, so the speech tokens are the same."],[15121,"Through the stack, `scripts/parity/cases/asr.jsonl` (seven clips, 4–120 s, English, Chinese and mixed, language detected or given) gives 0.1's transcripts exactly on the CPU in fp32 and on a GPU (an RTX 3090, 2026-10-19): language, text and every segment's timestamps."],[13030,"Through the whole stack on the GPU (`perf.py --device cuda:0`), best warm run:"],[15251,"Through the whole stac